  "result": "All 5 tests pass. I did not commit, since you didn't ask for one.\n\n**What I built:** `ledger/ledger.py` with the two exception classes and the `Ledger` class from the spec. Balances live in a single dict keyed by account id. Every mutating method validates the amount and looks up the accounts before touching state, so a failed withdraw or transfer leaves balances unchanged. Transfers to a self-account, boolean amounts, and non-int amounts are rejected as `ValueError`, matching the spirit of the non-negative-integer rule.\n\n**Approach chosen:** Approach B, snapshot state. Nothing in the fixed interface or tests needs an audit trail, replay, or time travel, so a plain id-to-balance mapping gives O(1) reads and the least code to get wrong, with atomicity handled by validating before mutating.",
